## The Hidden Halo Exception

[![Emerald cut diamond hidden halo ring in white gold — halo sits below girdle, ring reads as solitaire from above](/images/emerald-cut-diamond-hidden-halo-ring-white-gold.avif)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings?styleIDs=halo&ShapeID=Emerald&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

The hidden halo is the most counterintuitive option in this post and the most correct solution to The Outline Problem.

A standard halo sits around the girdle of the stone, level with or slightly above it, visible from the top. The hidden halo sits below the girdle — tucked underneath the stone, invisible from above. From the top, the ring looks like a solitaire.

From the side and at angles, the halo catches light and throws brilliance upward around the stone's perimeter.

For an emerald cut, this is ideal. The stone's rectangular outline is preserved completely — there is nothing around it to round, shorten, or compete with its geometry. The ring reads as a clean, architectural solitaire from the engagement position (hand extended, fingers together).

In motion and at angles, the hidden halo sparkle becomes visible. You get the halo effect without any halo geometry touching the stone's outline.

The [X Split Shank Hidden Halo in 14K White Gold at $3,040](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/x-split-shank-hidden-halo-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-white-gold-1-2-ct-tw-item-192267?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) adds the split shank detail — two bands that diverge toward the stone and then rejoin. Combined with the hidden halo below the girdle, the effect from above is a clean rectangular stone in an elegant split-shank solitaire. [14K Rose Gold is also available at $3,040](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/x-split-shank-hidden-halo-diamond-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-1-2-ct-tw-item-192269?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational).

With the [GIA 1ct D-VS1 at $3,140](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational), the complete hidden halo ring is [$6,180](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29333987?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). That is $30 more than the Falling Edge Platinum at $6,150.

The choice between them comes down to what the buyer values: maximum buyer-validated security (Falling Edge, 369 reviews) or maximum outline clarity (Hidden Halo, zero geometric conflict with the stone's shape). Both rings complete at essentially the same price. Neither is wrong — they solve The Outline Problem in different ways.

---

## Lab Stone + Halo: Complete Ring Prices

Lab-grown emerald cuts in halo settings deliver the most ring for the budget of any combination in certified jewelry. The halo setting adds 0.3–0.5ct of diamond sparkle around the stone. The lab center stone adds size and grade at a fraction of natural prices.

The result is a dramatically larger ring than any natural stone in the same setting at the same total price.

