The only thing the natural has that the lab does not is earth origin.

At the 2ct lab level, the comparison is even more stark. The [IGI 2ct D-FL at $4,735](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28828463?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) in a [Falling Edge Pavé Platinum setting at $3,010](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/falling-edge-pave-diamond-halo-engagement-ring-in-platinum-by-james-allen-item-311110?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) comes to $7,745 complete. That is a D-FL 2ct emerald cut in platinum with 369 reviews behind the setting. A natural 2ct D-VS1 emerald cut alone — without any setting — would exceed this total ring price by a significant margin. The lab combination at $7,745 is the most technically impressive ring build in this post on a per-carat basis.

> "Lab stones in halo settings benefit doubly from the combination. The halo's side diamonds are always lab or natural accents — that part is the same. But the center stone in lab at near-2ct faces up as a 9mm+ stone in the halo frame. In a natural 1ct halo at $4,660, you have a 7mm step-cut table surrounded by the halo. In a lab 1.94ct halo at $3,749, you have a 9mm step-cut table surrounded by the same halo. The difference is visible at arm's length." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**

